.** The key finding contradicts the regime-switch hypothesis:\n\n**What looked suspicious:** The group's *members* are sharply staggered in time. Members {323, 477, 865, 889, 1039, 1119} are active only in [246, ~14000] (202/202/140 events, then 3/2/1), while {1389, 1432, 1530, 1553, 1639, 1776} are essentially silent early (0/3/4) and active only after ~16000 (159/204/186) \u2014 step_10, step_11. The handover is abrupt: the early cohort drops to 0 by t=14246 and the late cohort jumps from 0 to 41 at t=16246 (step_12, step_13).\n\n**Why it is not a regime switch:** A regime switch requires an *old contact set giving way to a new one*. Instead, every member \u2014 early or late \u2014 interacts with the **identical fixed pool of the same 30 external nodes** [39, 55, 68, 118, 139, 306, 337, 425, 441, 585, 722, 788, 794, 901, 955, 977, 1008, 1009, 1057, 1145, 1147, 1181, 1247, 1387, 1444, 1702, 1725, 1734, 1840, 1847]. Pre-window 1039 (step_15) and post-window 1389 (step_16) return exactly this set; the 1-hop neighbourhoods are structurally identical, 31 nodes / 60 edges each (step_24, step_25). Jaccard overlap of pre- vs post-partner sets = **1.0** (step_28), confirmed at the far endpoints too (step_26, step_27).\n\n**Confounders ruled out:** Total volume is flat across the period (~120 events per 3000 units, step_03), so no volume artifact. The external contacts are equally active on both sides of the candidate change point (step_22, step_23), so the pool did not turn over. These contacts are not global hubs \u2014 they are absent from the top-activity list and carry only ~2\u20135 partners each (step_29, step_30), so the shared pool is group-specific, not a popularity artifact.\n\n**Conclusion:** What changes at t\u224815000 is *which group member is transmitting*, not *whom the group contacts*. Partner identity is perfectly conserved across the change point.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8b4a3bfa\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [323, 477, 865, 889, 1039, 1119, 1389, 1432, 1530, 1553, 1639, 1776],\n  \"window\": [246, 30246],\n  \"confidence\": 0.87,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_24\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_25\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_26\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_27\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_28\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_22\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_23\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_29\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_30\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"There is an abrupt temporal handover within the group, but it is a handover of active members, not of contacts. Members 323/477/865/889/1039/1119 are active only before t~14000 and 1389/1432/1530/1553/1639/1776 only after t~16000, with a near-silent gap in between. However, both cohorts interact with the exact same fixed pool of 30 external nodes; pre- and post-change partner sets have Jaccard overlap 1.0 and structurally identical 1-hop neighbourhoods (31 nodes, 60 edges). A regime switch requires old contacts being replaced by new ones, which does not occur. Confounders controlled: event volume is flat across the period, the external contacts are equally active on both sides of the candidate change point, and they are low-degree non-hubs, so neither volume nor popularity explains the pattern. Decision: absent.\"\n}\n```", "total_